// A call that cannot work, caught before the program runs.
//
// The interpreter has always reported this (`RT036`), but only on the line that
// actually executed. Now that a function's parameters are known statically —
// and, since the standard library declares its signatures, so are every
// built-in's — the count can be checked without running anything, so a wrong
// call on a branch that never runs is still a wrong call.
fn add(a, b) { a + b }
print(add(1))
